1
00:00:02,133 --> 00:00:04,829
So these requests here don't seem to work

2
00:00:04,909 --> 00:00:06,642
correctly. Specifically here for the

3
00:00:06,754 --> 00:00:09,498
password, I get some email-specific error

4
00:00:09,498 --> 00:00:11,616
message if I'm entering an incorrect

5
00:00:11,664 --> 00:00:12,113
password.

6
00:00:13,284 --> 00:00:15,803
Now, as I explained in the last lecture, in

7
00:00:15,996 --> 00:00:19,044
theory, this validation code here in this

8
00:00:19,108 --> 00:00:22,814
POST route should output a password-specific

9
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:25,943
error message if I have password data in the

10
00:00:25,943 --> 00:00:28,526
request body and the password is too short.

11
00:00:29,154 --> 00:00:30,869
But clearly that doesn't work.

12
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:36,019
And the only reason why that might not work

13
00:00:36,099 --> 00:00:39,354
is that maybe here when we entered a

14
00:00:39,402 --> 00:00:40,321
password value,

15
00:00:41,029 --> 00:00:44,752
this email value is part of that POST

16
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:45,541
request

17
00:00:46,492 --> 00:00:48,700
because if that would be the case, this if

18
00:00:48,829 --> 00:00:50,266
check here would become active

19
00:00:51,271 --> 00:00:53,913
because I'm checking if I got an email field

20
00:00:53,946 --> 00:00:54,967
in the request body

21
00:00:55,631 --> 00:00:58,031
and I then validate that email address and I

22
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,454
don't even run my other checks here if

23
00:01:00,454 --> 00:01:01,229
that's the case.

24
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:04,958
So it seems like we don't even get to this

25
00:01:04,958 --> 00:01:07,444
check and that can only happen if there is

26
00:01:07,638 --> 00:01:10,743
some email data in the request body. So that

27
00:01:10,743 --> 00:01:11,904
seems to be the problem here.

28
00:01:13,652 --> 00:01:15,525
Now, of course, the POST request for that

29
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:17,817
password is sent whenever that password

30
00:01:17,914 --> 00:01:21,611
input changes. So why is there email data

31
00:01:21,643 --> 00:01:23,015
attached to that request?

32
00:01:24,305 --> 00:01:27,353
Well, because when HTMX sends a POST

33
00:01:27,353 --> 00:01:29,256
request, it tries to attach any meaningful

34
00:01:29,256 --> 00:01:29,934
data it can get.

35
00:01:32,953 --> 00:01:36,096
And if that POST request is sent because

36
00:01:36,129 --> 00:01:39,062
some input changes and that input is in a

37
00:01:39,062 --> 00:01:39,499
form,

38
00:01:40,163 --> 00:01:43,540
it turns out that HTMX gathers all the form

39
00:01:43,572 --> 00:01:45,138
data of the entire form.

40
00:01:46,235 --> 00:01:49,075
And that's of course not what I want here.

41
00:01:49,607 --> 00:01:52,262
I wanna attach just the data of this input,

42
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:54,241
not the entire form data.

43
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:57,890
So what can we do about that?

44
00:01:59,445 --> 00:02:03,644
Now, you might recall the hx-vals attribute

45
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:06,293
from earlier in the course, which could be

46
00:02:06,293 --> 00:02:09,710
used to add new data to an outgoing request.

47
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:14,231
Now, I essentially wanna filter the data

48
00:02:14,312 --> 00:02:16,859
that's added automatically so that not all

49
00:02:16,989 --> 00:02:18,173
the values are added.

50
00:02:19,518 --> 00:02:21,552
And this can be achieved with help of

51
00:02:21,552 --> 00:02:26,166
another HTMX attribute. The hx-params

52
00:02:26,426 --> 00:02:26,897
attribute.

53
00:02:28,635 --> 00:02:31,558
This attribute allows you to filter the

54
00:02:31,883 --> 00:02:33,887
parameters, the values

55
00:02:34,474 --> 00:02:36,853
that will be attached to the outgoing

56
00:02:36,853 --> 00:02:37,359
request.

57
00:02:38,986 --> 00:02:41,831
To be precise, here with hx-params,

58
00:02:42,361 --> 00:02:45,479
you can specify a comma-separated list of

59
00:02:45,592 --> 00:02:47,923
the values that should be added to the

60
00:02:47,923 --> 00:02:48,327
request.

61
00:02:49,297 --> 00:02:51,948
And any other values which you don't specify

62
00:02:52,028 --> 00:02:53,305
here will be dropped and will not be part of

63
00:02:53,305 --> 00:02:53,580
the request.

64
00:02:56,671 --> 00:02:58,908
So here on that password input, I for

65
00:02:58,908 --> 00:03:01,305
example wanna make sure that I only send

66
00:03:01,305 --> 00:03:02,769
along the password value.

67
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:06,063
So the value that's stored under a name

68
00:03:06,127 --> 00:03:08,410
password in the outgoing request. And that's

69
00:03:08,410 --> 00:03:10,757
achieved by simply adding that value name

70
00:03:11,146 --> 00:03:13,848
password in this case to Ajax params.

71
00:03:15,946 --> 00:03:18,124
Now if I would also want to include the

72
00:03:18,124 --> 00:03:20,253
email, I could add it comma separated like

73
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:20,560
this,

74
00:03:21,205 --> 00:03:23,722
though that of course is absolutely not what

75
00:03:23,722 --> 00:03:25,351
I want here. So instead it's just Ajax

76
00:03:25,351 --> 00:03:25,545
params password.

77
00:03:28,389 --> 00:03:31,182
And we can achieve the opposite effect here

78
00:03:31,359 --> 00:03:31,824
on the email

79
00:03:32,563 --> 00:03:35,179
input by setting hx-params to email.

80
00:03:36,351 --> 00:03:38,887
That makes sure that here it's really only

81
00:03:39,064 --> 00:03:41,295
the email value that's part of the outgoing

82
00:03:41,327 --> 00:03:41,825
request.

83
00:03:42,932 --> 00:03:45,100
Well, and with that done, if you go back to

84
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:47,700
the application and reload after saving all

85
00:03:47,812 --> 00:03:48,358
your files,

86
00:03:48,952 --> 00:03:51,151
if I enter an invalid email, that's still

87
00:03:51,264 --> 00:03:51,825
validated.

88
00:03:52,869 --> 00:03:55,133
But if I then enter an invalid password, and

89
00:03:55,133 --> 00:03:57,598
then tab out of this field, now you see I'm

90
00:03:57,695 --> 00:03:59,710
getting that password error message.

91
00:04:01,095 --> 00:04:03,158
And that's therefore how you can control

92
00:04:03,593 --> 00:04:06,139
which data is attached to the outgoing

93
00:04:06,172 --> 00:04:06,655
request.
